 \begin{abstract}
-Eviction ends tag residency before a dirty line's obligations end: data must survive until downstream acceptance, and address ordering may persist until writeback completion. A fixed-partition L2 bridges these lifetimes by copying unchanged bytes into a writeback buffer. EP-L2 instead transfers ownership: the victim retains its original banked slot, the incoming resident obtains a distinct legal landing, and local data and ordering state retire separately. At equal tag count, payload capacity, and modeled bank service, five complete non-atomic workload pairs eliminate all resident-to-writeback copies but yield only a 1.001600 geometric-mean speed ratio, including one slowdown. Under matched downstream controls, neither design records local-buffer refusals, yet U-WB's relative performance does not uniformly improve. These results distinguish reduced internal work from application progress; competitive static allocation and physical implementation cost remain unresolved.
+Eviction ends tag residency before a dirty line's obligations end: data must survive until downstream acceptance, and address ordering may persist until writeback completion. A fixed-partition L2 bridges these lifetimes by copying unchanged bytes into a writeback buffer. EP-L2 instead transfers ownership: the victim retains its original banked slot, the incoming resident obtains a distinct legal landing, and local data and ordering state retire separately. At equal tag count, payload capacity, and modeled bank service, five complete non-atomic workload pairs eliminate all resident-to-writeback copies but yield only a 1.001600 geometric-mean speed ratio, including one slowdown. Under matched queue/service controls for Convolution/FWT, both designs show no WB-data admission or L2-to-DRAM queue refusals; WAD/scheduler pressure can persist and relative performance does not uniformly improve. These results distinguish reduced internal work from application progress; competitive static allocation and physical implementation cost remain unresolved.
 \end{abstract}
 
